 stronger TMED10 dependency, is also weak and deferral is appropriate. H16, the uncertain KRAS-mutation association with RIT1 dependency, has a small point estimate and an upper 95% bound below the approximate 10% clinical-significance threshold, so I reject it as unsupported in this dataset and close it.\n\nThe main critical concern remains multiplicity and post hoc reversal for H22. H22 was registered after observing that the original canonical KEAP1/NFE2L2 direction in H6 was opposite to the observed effect. That makes H22 susceptible to discovery artifact, but the effect is enormous and now appears robust in adenocarcinoma and high-quality screens. Because H22 is the most biologically surprising accepted claim and has not yet received independent validation, I request validation for H22. If it survives independent validation, the dataset-specific claim that KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency will be much more secure. If it fails, I will downgrade H22 to unresolved or reject it despite the original discovery signal. All accepted effects remain limited to this supplied dataset, not causal inference or external generalization.",
