  "narrative": "All estimates below are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction, so a positive signed estimate supports the corresponding claim. Two evidence items must be assessed at synthesis: the H22 response deadline and the new independent validation of H9.\n\nH22, KEAP1 loss associated with much weaker NFE2L2 dependency, is strongly and independently confirmed. Discovery R6 gave a signed estimate of about +0.796 with interval about +0.776 to +0.816; independent validation R26 gives about +0.810 with independent interval about +0.780 to +0.839. On the dependency scale, this is far larger than the approximate 10% relative-effect benchmark and the validation interval remains entirely on the same side. I therefore accept H22 and keep its investigation closed. The canonical claim H6, KEAP1 loss associated with stronger NFE2L2 dependency, remains rejected and closed because the opposite direction is supported in both discovery and validation.\n\nH9, MSI-high status associated with much stronger WRN dependency, now has direct independent validation. Discovery R9 gave signed about +0.790, interval about +0.760 to +0.819. Validation R53 gives signed about +0.822 with independent interval about +0.768 to +0.875. This is an enormous association on the WRN dependency scale and clearly exceeds the 10% relative benchmark. Together with the earlier adenocarcinoma, high-screen-ROC, high-Cas9, higher-mutation-burden, and co-alteration-exclusion refinements, the evidence strongly supports a dataset-specific association between MSI-high status and markedly stronger WRN dependency. I accept H9 and close its investigation.\n\nThe overall supported picture in this supplied lung cell-line dataset is dominated by four observational associations. SMARCA4 loss is associated with markedly stronger SMARCA2 dependency (H5 accepted and active; H19, H31, H44, H51, H55, and H61 provide strong robustness, and H31 is validated in the high-screen-ROC stratum). TP53 loss is associated with markedly weaker USP7 dependency (H8 accepted/closed and independently validated). MSI-high status is associated with markedly stronger WRN dependency (H9 accepted/closed and independently validated). KEAP1 loss is associated with markedly weaker NFE2L2 dependency (H22 accepted/closed and independently validated). These are within-dataset associations, not causal statements or claims of external generalizability. The robustness and validation work reduces concern about low screen quality, low Cas9 activity, broad co-alteration confounding, or minor lineage strata, but unmeasured linked genomic states could still contribute.\n\nNegative and specificity findings remain important. BRAF V600E does not support stronger BRAF dependency (H2 rejected/closed). The canonical KEAP1-NFE2L2 direction is rejected (H6). MYC amplification does not support stronger DHX9, RPL11, or POLR2A dependency (H10-H12 rejected). KRAS mutation does not support altered RIT1 dependency (H16 rejected). ARID1A loss does not support stronger SMARCA2 dependency among SMARCA4-intact lines (H43 rejected), while the SMARCA4 effect remains large among ARID1A-intact lines (H44 accepted). SMARCA4 loss does not support stronger POLR2A dependency (H68 rejected), supporting SMARCA2-specificity rather than a broad transcriptional vulnerability. TP53 loss does not support stronger MDM2 dependency in the high-screen-ROC stratum (H67 rejected).\n\nThe main unresolved signals involve KRAS and some weak RTK/driver associations. The original NF1-loss claim among KRAS-wild-type lines was rejected because the raw effect favored weaker KRAS dependency, not stronger KRAS dependency. The reversed post hoc claim H69, NF1 loss associated with weaker KRAS dependency among KRAS-wild-type lines, has signed estimate about +0.039 with interval about +0.006 to +0.071. This is statistically positive and the point estimate is around the 10% relative benchmark, but the lower interval is well below 10% and the claim was generated after observing the reversal, so I keep it unresolved and active. KRAS mutation itself remains only weakly positive in high-Cas9 screens (H65 signed +0.030, interval +0.004 to +0.056), also below a decisive 10% lower bound, and remains unresolved. EGFR amplification remains unresolved after validation: discovery R3 was borderline and validation R13 is smaller and crosses null, with point estimate below a convincing clinically meaningful benchmark. ERBB2/STAT3 and MDM2 high-Cas9 follow-ups are also unresolved or weak. WRN-BRCA2 extensions do not currently support a broad DNA-repair extension of MSI-driven WRN dependency, but their intervals are not tight enough for definitive rejection.\n\nThe most informative next analyses should refine the two active KRAS signals in the cleanest available strata: NF1 loss among KRAS-wild-type lines and KRAS mutation itself, both under high screen ROC. H70 asks whether the post hoc NF1-loss weaker-KRAS signal persists in high-quality screens. H71 asks whether the weak KRAS-mutation stronger-dependency signal becomes decisive in high-quality screens. I do not yet accept H69 because it is post hoc and its uncertainty interval does not robustly exceed the 10% clinical-significance benchmark.",
