  "narrative": "All intervals are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction, so positive signed values support the registered direction. H123 now has independent validation. For PTEN-loss versus PTEN-intact lines with high IFN-gamma signature, the discovery signed association with stronger dependency_STAT3 was +0.423 (interval +0.319 to +0.527). The independent adjusted validation signed effect was +0.193 with lower bound +0.045 and upper bound +0.340. Using the approximate 10% population-scale benchmark for STAT3 of about +0.028, the validated lower bound is above the clinical-meaningfulness threshold, so I accept and close H123. The validation effect is materially attenuated relative to discovery, which is consistent with some context inflation in the high-IFN-gamma discovery estimate, but the independent interval still supports a scientifically meaningful dataset-specific PTEN-loss/STAT3 association in that context. I am closing H151 and H152 as unresolved because their signed lower bounds (+0.011 and +0.008, respectively) are below the relevant 10% benchmarks and validation requests are exhausted. I am also closing H165 as unresolved because the reversed EGFR-amplification/KRAS-mutant EGFR-dependency signal is post hoc and the lower bound is too close to zero for confident acceptance. The supported dataset-specific core remains: SMARCA4 loss is associated with markedly stronger SMARCA2 dependency; KEAP1 loss is associated with markedly weaker NFE2L2 dependency; TP53 loss is associated with markedly weaker USP7 dependency; MSI-high status is associated with markedly stronger WRN dependency; and PTEN loss is associated with stronger STAT3 dependency across broad and several constrained contexts, now including the validated high-IFN-gamma context. Negative or unsupported findings include clinically meaningful null results for most receptor/MAPK and MYC-amplification hypotheses, no canonical TP53-loss/MDM2 effect, no stable PTEN-loss effect on KRAS dependency among KRAS-mutant lines, no supported MSI-high effect on POLR2A, and no meaningful constructed-marker effects. The most informative next analyses are robustness checks in technically stringent screens for the accepted PTEN-loss/STAT3 contexts (high IFN, high EMT, and high stemness) and for TP53-loss/USP7 in high AXL expression, while retaining active complement/context tests for PTEN-loss/STAT3 under high MYC or high AXL and MSI-high/WRN under TP53 loss. All conclusions remain observational within the supplied dataset and do not establish causality or generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 11 synthesis. H123 validated: PTEN-loss/STAT3 in high IFN remains above 10% benchmark after attenuation, accepted and closed. H151/H152 marginal interactions closed unresolved; H165 post-hoc EGFR reversal closed unresolved due near-zero lower bound. Core accepted associations: SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2, KEAP1-loss/weaker-NFE2L2, TP53-loss/weaker-USP7, MSI-high/WRN, PTEN-loss/stronger-STAT3. Negative: KRAS mutation effects, BRAF/EGFR/ERBB2 marginal effects, MYC amplification outcomes, TP53/MDM2, PTEN/KRAS in KRAS mutants, MSI/POLR2A, research markers. No validation budget remains. Next plan: register QC-robustness refinements for PTEN-loss/STAT3 in high IFN/EMT/stemness and TP53-loss/USP7 in high AXL, and retain active H155/H156/H162 context tests. Maintain causal and generalizability cautions."
